  "overview": "The Zero W shrinks the Raspberry Pi down to credit-card-sliver size and a fraction of the price of a Pi 4, while still booting the same full Linux OS and exposing the same 40-pin GPIO layout. The processor is much slower (a single ARM11 core versus the Pi 4's quad-core), so it's a poor fit for anything computer-vision-heavy, but it's the board of choice for projects that need 'a real Linux computer' in the smallest, cheapest possible form — USB gadget emulation, low-power sensor hubs, or retro handhelds. The GPIO header usually ships unpopulated (bare holes, no pins) and needs to be soldered before use.",
  "simpleSummary": "A shrunk-down, cheaper Raspberry Pi computer that still runs the full Linux OS.",
